    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d684">

                <pb facs="0649" n="641" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg641"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.93/435: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-02-27T17:00:00+09:00">February 27, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 27—7:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>145. My British colleague<anchor n="45" xml:id="d684-f1"/> tells me that in
                    conversation yesterday with the Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs, Tani said
                    that the United States is aiding Chiang Kai-shek “in many different ways” and
                    that the Japanese Government is greatly concerned. Craigie said that we had done
                    nothing beyond granting a loan but Tani maintained that we are supporting Chiang
                    in various other ways although he did not specify in what ways. Craigie says
                    that Tani’s concern appeared to be genuine and that he gave every indication of
                    being “greatly upset”.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d684-f1" n="45" xml:id="d684-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Robert L. Craigie, British Ambassador in Japan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
